  "Number": 45,
  "Question": "The number of diagonals that can be drawn in a polygon of \\(100\\) sides is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(4850\\)",
    "B": "\\(4950\\)",
    "C": "\\(9900\\)",
    "D": "\\(98\\)",
    "E": "\\(8800\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "A diagonal joins two nonadjacent vertices. From any one vertex, exclude that vertex itself and its two neighbors. This leaves\n\\[\n100-3=97\n\\]\npossible diagonal endpoints. Doing this at all \\(100\\) vertices gives \\(100\\cdot97\\) counts.\n\nEvery diagonal appears in this count, and each appears exactly twice, once from each of its two endpoints. Dividing by two therefore gives\n\\[\n\\frac{100\\cdot97}{2}=\\boxed{4850}.\n\\]\nThe answer is choice A.",
